    "description": "The Query Service validates request fields against a versioned schema registry shared by many request threads. Parsing a schema and compiling its validators takes about 80 ms, but once compiled the validator is immutable and reads are extremely frequent. The first request for a given schema version should create it on demand; later requests must reuse the same instance, and two simultaneous first requests must never publish competing validators. The registry should avoid taking the global registry lock on every validation, while still preventing readers from observing a partially constructed validator. Constructors must be idempotent, and a failed build may be retried without exposing an invalid result. The decision is whether to pay for eager initialization during service startup or use a lazy, safely published initialization path that keeps the hot read path inexpensive.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose how to initialize one expensive validator per schema version exactly once while allowing concurrent readers to proceed without locking on every access.",
    "notes": "Double-checked-locking fits lazy exactly-once validator construction with a lock-free hot read path and safe publication; guardian-object is the near-miss (encapsulated guarded initialization) and thread-local-storage supports per-thread read-path caching."
  },
